'''Sahra of Galwa''' (c. 1675 – 1740) was the Regent of the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|Kingdom of Galwa]] from 1709 until her death in 1740. The daughter of [[Faarax of Galwa|Lord Faarax of Galwa]], granddaughter of [[Nuur III]], and mother of [[Cali IV of Galwa|Cali IV]], Sahra governed the western Burawi kingdom during its formative period following the [[Burawa Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709.

Sahra assumed the regency following the death of [[Andrias III]] and the division of the Burawi kingdom. Although her father, the aged Lord Faarax, was initially expected to serve as regent for the infant Cali IV, his incapacity left Sahra — who had been exercising effective authority — to assume formal control. She established the Galwa court at [[Hargiesa]], promulgated the [[New Laws of 1710]] that centralised the kingdom's administration, and maintained neutrality during the [[First War of the Isthmus]].

== Early life ==

Sahra was born around 1675 in [[Galwa]], the daughter of [[Faarax of Galwa|Lord Faarax of Galwa]], a senior member of the [[Kadim-Galwa|Kadim-Galwa branch]] of the Burawi royal family. Her father was a descendant of [[Nuur III]] and held substantial influence within the western provinces of the unified Burawi kingdom.

Sahra married [[Aadan of Fayeha]] around 1695. Aadan was a lord from the Fayeha branch of the Burawi family — a minor sub-branch of the royal line associated with the [[Fayeha]] region on the [[Great Kaftian Lake]]. The marriage strengthened ties between the Galwa and Fayeha branches of the Kadim dynasty.

Sahra gave birth to three daughters — their names are not recorded in surviving sources — before conceiving a son late in her childbearing years. [[Cali IV]] was born around 1709, when Sahra was approximately thirty-four years old — an age considered advanced for a first son in that era. The birth of a male heir after three daughters was regarded as auspicious within the Galwa court.

== Assumption of power ==

By the early 1700s, Sahra's father, Lord Faarax, had become elderly and incapacitated. Sahra, who had accompanied him in administrative matters, effectively assumed responsibility for the Galwa faction's affairs. When Andrias III died in 1709 and the succession crisis erupted, the Galwa faction initially proposed that Lord Faarax serve as regent for the infant Cali IV. His incapacity, however, made this arrangement impossible in practice.

The succession crisis pitted the Galwa faction, backing the child Cali IV, against the Kaysoor faction, backing the adult claimant Iacob V. Sahra led the Galwa faction through negotiations mediated by the [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]], ultimately agreeing to the division of the kingdom. Under the settlement, Cali IV became king of the western portion with Sahra as regent, while Iacob V ruled the eastern portion as the [[Kingdom of Burawa]].

== Regency (1709–1740) ==

{{main|Kingdom of Galwa (1709–1743)}}

=== Establishment of the court ===

Following the split, Sahra established the Galwa court at [[Hargiesa]], a city on the western coast that offered strategic distance from the disputed border with the eastern kingdom and access to [[Aethiopean Ocean]] trade routes. The court, drawn from officials loyal to the Galwa faction, became the centre of the new kingdom's administration.

=== The New Laws of 1710 ===

{{main|New Laws of 1710}}

In 1710, Sahra promulgated the [[New Laws of 1710]], a comprehensive legal code that established the administrative framework of the kingdom. The laws created a two-tier system of fourteen provinces and 115 counties, each governed by administrators appointed by the imperial court. The centralisation stripped the Hoga aristocracy of feudal prerogatives and concentrated appointment power in the regent.

=== Neutrality and foreign policy ===

Sahra maintained neutrality during the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716), preserving Galwa's resources while the eastern kingdom committed fully to the Illypnian coalition. The decision foreclosed colonial opportunities but allowed the regency to focus on consolidating the new kingdom.

Relations with the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] remained complex. The Church had supported the Galwa claim during the crisis but shifted to neutrality following the split. Sahra accepted the [[Cardinal of Burawa]]'s intermediary role, recognising that a schism would destabilise the kingdom, but the relationship remained cooler than either party preferred.

=== Colonial governance ===

The colonial protectorate of [[Bindorama]] posed the greatest challenge to the regency. In the 1720s, Sahra's government implemented measures favouring Galwa-aligned elites in the protectorate's legislative assembly, violating the autonomy guarantees of the 1709 treaty. The policies provoked opposition from Burawi loyalists and indigenous communities, escalating into the [[Bindorama Civil War]] by 1735.

== Death ==

Sahra died in 1740 at approximately sixty-five years of age. Her death ended the regency; Cali IV, then around thirty-one years old, assumed direct rule as king. Sahra was interred in the royal crypt at Hargiesa.

== Legacy ==

Sahra's regency established the institutions and governance structures of the Kingdom of Galwa. The New Laws of 1710 remained the administrative foundation of the kingdom for decades. Her absolutist governance contrasted with the parliamentarism of the eastern kingdom, creating distinct political traditions in the two Burawi states.

The Bindorama Civil War, which began during her regency and concluded under her son's rule, resulted in the loss of Galwa's colonial protectorate and the creation of [[Asikyira]]. The conflict underscored the limits of the new kingdom's capacity to project power.

